  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, I grew up in Green County, Yellow Springs, and my family was in the wholesale seed business. A company started by my grandparents in the 1920s, a feed store downtown, Yellow Springs, and then my time dad came home from World War II. It was moving into a seed company. And I worked there as a kid, worked there during college and even into law school when I was working summers. And it was great to start off in a small business because I think you have a real understanding of what small businessmen and women have to do. I started my political career as a county prosecuting attorney. When you're a county prosecutor, you kind of see all the problems in your community. It was a great, great, great experience, you know, was in the state senate. Then, you know, had the opportunity to serve in the United States Congress for eight years, four years as George Vornavitch's lieutenant governor. And then after that, in the United States Senate, of course, for the last eight years, I've been the Attorney General of the State of Ohio. So I'm very proud of my ability really to pull people together. I think sometimes there's a great concern about what's going on in Washington, whether or not people can get along and get anything done. And I think that's a real legitimate concern. But I've had throughout my career, the ability to actually pull people together, Democrats, Republicans, independents, and actually get things done. And more than happy to talk more about that as we move forward.",
