  },
  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think government should do what we can't do for ourselves collectively and government should focus on, you know, really, really the basic things, basic things are keeping us safe. You know, I think you have to, you have to start with that. We also look to government to help us, you know, educate our kids. It's the primary responsibility of the parents, but You know, we have a great public school system in the state of Ohio. Fran and I were both educated in our public schools and, you know, our grandkids and some of our kids and some of our grandkids go to Cedarville, which is a very small school district, rural school district in the eastern part of Green County. I think we have only about 650 kids, K through 12. So, you know, government is there to do the essential things for us. I think sometimes we look to government to do too much. I think we have to, you know, protect individuals' rights and individual freedom. So that's kind of the essential function, function of government. And I just, maybe if I could go back to kind of the previous question, I want to mentioned a little my personal history, you know, I met my wife Fran in first grade and We we started started dating acts. I had a first date in seventh grade and really started dating in in high school and then You know we both went to Miami University and we we got married between our second and third year at at Miami and You know, I had two kids before we left college. We now have eight children and 20, 23 grandchildren waiting for our birth of our 24th grandchild in January. So we're very excited about that.",
