  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "one of the things that I've asked John to do is to really lead our effort to reinvent state government, really with innovation. Government historically is slow. It doesn't keep up. It doesn't use innovation. It does not use technology as much as the private sector. And we got to change that. We got to shake things up. And so I've asked John Houston to head that up. We're going to put a working group together of people who really change agents, people from the business community, people who understand technology. We look at state government as all about customer service. I'll go back again to my background in the seed business. My grandmother ran the office. She was the one who answered the phone. She was the one that made the sale. She was the one that took care of customers. She was all about customer service. I learned that in that business. I've tried throughout my career to You know, take that to my job in government. For example, we run the state crime lab as the attorney general. Our customers are the police, the prosecutors, and the sheriffs around the state. And so we have looked at our job as to service them and by helping them through our crime lab and through investigators, we were able to help the people of the state.",
