  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Look, I think you have to start with education. It all comes back to education. We want for every child in this state what we want for our own kids and that is that they be able to live up to their god-given potential. We want every kid who graduates from high school. First of all, we want everybody to graduate from high school. Second, when they graduate from high school, we want them to have a real pathway to a career, a good paying job, or be truly ready for college. To do that, though, we have to start early. We have some kids who are growing up who are growing up in Ohio through no fall of their own in very difficult circumstances. And we've got to really put a lot more focus on early childhood education, early childhood development. So when the child gets to kindergarten, They're not way way behind. They're at the starting line. We can get them to the starting line and then we go from there. I think besides early childhood education, we're also going to really focus on our career centers. We have some great career centers. We have some great two-year colleges out there. that really are doing a very, very good job. Our goal should be to take our career centers, and I would just say to any of our listeners, if you've not been into your local career center, your high school career center, we used to call them vocational schools, you need to go in because you're going to really be surprised at what they're doing, doing amazing work and preparing young people for 21st century jobs, and not just young people. We have them, you know, if you look at what goes on in communities with both the Career centers and the two-year colleges, the two-year colleges are doing a good job in preparing, not just young people, but people who are in mid-career as well. So we're going to focus a lot on them. We're also going to focus on what we call nano degrees or micro degrees. And this has to do with people who are in mid-career, but who want to improve their skill sets. We need to strategically look at what Ohio needs to move forward, whether it's more coders, more machinists. whatever it happens to be, and then we need to focus on making sure we have people who have those degrees or who have those skill sets, those industry credentials. I'll just give you an example. You can take someone who is working on the factory floor making, let's say, $17, $18 an hour in three weeks at night. They can be trained to be someone who can take care and fix a robot and robots. And when you do that and they get that industry credential, they can go from $18 an hour to $28, $29 an hour. It is good for them. It opens up the job that they just left for somebody else and enables companies to move forward. So that type of strategic planning, I think, is very important.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Education should be the starting point for improving outcomes for children in Ohio.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Every child in Ohio deserves to reach their god-given potential.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "High school graduation should be universal for all students in Ohio.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "All high school graduates should have access to a clear pathway toward either a career or college readiness.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Early childhood education is essential to ensure children enter kindergarten prepared.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Some children in Ohio face significant challenges due to circumstances beyond their control.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Career centers and two-year colleges in Ohio are currently performing effectively.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Career centers and two-year colleges serve both youth and adult learners, including mid-career professionals.",
