  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and first of all, let me just quickly talk about what we have done in the attorney general's office. When I became attorney general, We were already well into an opiate epidemic and we Started to close the pill mills that were particularly in Southern, Ohio. We have closed them In the first year we closed every pill mill for example in sciota County, which was the worst hit County We have taken the licenses of over a hundred doctors who were nothing more frankly than drug dealers over 20 pharmacists who were just bad bad actors and We have made some significant inroads, but we have a ways to go, and we all certainly know that. One of the things that I have done is sue the drug companies. I sue them because, frankly, they're responsible for the beginning of this drug epidemic in Ohio. They're the ones who told the doctors that these new miracle drugs, these opiates, were not very addictive. And one, in fact, we know they're very, very, very addictive. As we look forward, we have a 12-point plan. Again, I would invite our listeners to go up on our webpage, mikedewine.com, look at our 12-point plan. Let me mention a couple of them. Number one, it's important that we do something that we're not really doing in Ohio very much, and that is prevention. I'm governor in every school, every child, beginning in kindergarten, K through 12, 13 straight years, Every year there's going to be something that will help them avoid going down the path of addiction. It's what the educators call social emotional learning. It's about good decision-making. It's about health. It's about wellness. If we do that and do something that's age-appropriate every year and also do something every single year that is proven to actually work, what we will find is that in the long run we will fundamentally change addiction in the state of Ohio and the number of people who are addicts. We're also going to double down on treatment. Our drug courts are working exceedingly well in Ohio. We have to have more of them. It's really tough love, but these judges are doing a really phenomenal job in that area as well. And the other part is the law enforcement. We have a model that we're using in Ohio, but we have to now cover the entire state. We're only covering a part of the state. And our goal is to go after the Mexican drug cartels who are bringing this poison into Ohio. The fentanyl, which is killing most people, comes in from Mexico. It originates in China. The Mexican drug dealers bring it into Ohio. And so we've got to double down on our efforts to go after these drug dealers.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
