  {
    "episode_id": 80456,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80456.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "Well, throughout my career, I've been able to pull people together. Look, this election is going to be over with shortly, and I believe I'm going to be the governor. And I will start right after the election to start pulling people together. Election campaigns can be divisive. One of the reasons that we put an ad up with our daughter, our youngest daughter, and our youngest of our eight children is because we kind of wanted to put something that was positive up there and talk about my vision for the future of the State of Ohio. If you look at my record, I'm the one person running for governor who has a long record of being able to work across party lines. Let me give you an example, a couple examples. As the attorney general of the state, I have worked with the Democrat sheriff in Lucas County to help get drug treatment to people who have gone afoul of the law. I have worked with Tim McGinty, who is a prosecuting attorney in Cuyahoga County, again, a Democrat, and worked with him very, very closely as we have taken on this old rape kit problem. And as a direct result of that, just in Cuyahoga County alone, they've had over 700 indictments of rape us. So again, I serve every single day, Democrat sheriffs, Democrat prosecutors, Democrat corners throughout the state of Ohio. And we don't ask what their party is. We don't really care. We just go get the job done. And so that's what I'm going to do as governor.",
